# caos-tools/hello-go: the smallest tool a client repo can carry, and the
# pattern for any other Go tool added here. One Go file curried onto caos'
# std/go worker image, which `go run`s it inside a module that provides
# `caos/w` (see worker.go).
#
# `--base:@=DEEP-DEPS/go` names the image by a LOCAL deep-deps mount, declared
# in the sibling DEPS as `../../caos-std/go`. An expression is evaluated
# against its own directory and cannot climb out of it (`../../caos-std/go`
# here fails with "base path not found in tree"), so the dependency has to be
# mounted INTO this directory first — which is what the root .caos-expr's
# deep-deps line does, over the tree with caos-std/ already spliced in.
# Nothing of caos is copied here.
#
# A reader with no `@in`: its ArgTree is its arguments and nothing else, so
# `@call` adds the tool call's id to make each call its own run instead of a
# cache hit on the first one.
HELP=<<END
Say hello. A smoke test that this client repo's own caos-tools/ build and run:
run_tool(path="caos-tools/hello-go", arguments={"name": "caos"}).
@param [name] Who to greet; defaults to "world".
@call
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/go --worker1:@=worker.go --help=$HELP
